
Invocation for different real life situation

To understand this concept effortlessly, let us imagine a real-life scenario: Dining at a busy, high-end restaurant.
In this analogy:
    • The Customer’s Order is the HTTP Request trying to reach the Kitchen (the Controller).
    • The Waiter is your JwtFilter.
    • The Restaurant Manager is Mockito (the testing framework).

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------------
        The Real-Life Restaurant Scenario
--------------------------------------------------------------------- */

When a customer sits down and orders food, that order has to pass through a sequence of staff members before the chef in the kitchen can actually cook it.

    1. The Real Production Setup (How it works when the app is running normally)

        1. The customer places an order (Request).
        2. The Waiter (JwtFilter) intercepts the order.
        3. The waiter checks the order to see if it has a valid VIP Voucher (JWT Token).
        4. If it looks good, the waiter passes the order ticket down the line to the kitchen expeditor (chain.doFilter(request, response)).
        5. The food gets cooked, and the customer eats (200 OK).

    2. The Broken Test Setup (What happened before your fix)

        When you wrote your slice test and used @MockitoBean private JwtFilter jwtFilter;, you told the Restaurant Manager (Mockito): "For this test, replace my real, active waiter with a cardboard cutout of a waiter."
            1. The customer hands their order ticket to the cardboard waiter.
            2. The cardboard waiter just stands there. It has no brain. It doesn't check the token, but more importantly, it does not hand the ticket to the kitchen expeditor.
            3. The order is stuck in the cardboard waiter's hand forever. The kitchen never receives it.
            4. Because the chain was broken right at the start, the restaurant manager (Mockito) steps in, grabs an empty plate, and hands it back to the customer just to clear the table. The customer gets a blank response (200 OK), bypassing the security check entirely!

    *Where invocation and doAnswer Step In:

        To fix the cardboard waiter, you added the doAnswer setup block.
        In restaurant terms, you walked up to the Restaurant Manager (Mockito) and said: "Listen, whenever a customer hands a ticket to this cardboard waiter, I want you to immediately freeze time, read the clipboard of what just happened, and manually pass that ticket down the line yourself."
        Here is exactly how that plays out in your code block:

        /* ---------------------------------------------------------------------

        doAnswer(invocation -> {
            // 1. Time Freezes! You look at Mockito's clipboard (invocation).
            // 2. You look at the items that were handed to the cardboard waiter:
            HttpServletRequest request   = invocation.getArgument(0); // The Customer's Order
            HttpServletResponse response = invocation.getArgument(1); // The Customer's Plate
            FilterChain chain            = invocation.getArgument(2); // The Kitchen Expeditor Line

            // 3. You manually push the order down the line to the next person!
            chain.doFilter(request, response);

            return null;
        }).when(jwtFilter).doFilter(any(), any(), any());

        The Visual Summary

            • when(jwtFilter).doFilter(any(), any(), any()): "When someone drops off a ticket to the cardboard waiter (which expects an Order, a Plate, and a Line)..."
            • invocation: This is the manager's live clipboard. It lets you inspect the exact Order, Plate, and Line that were just used at that fraction of a second.
            • chain.doFilter(request, response): This is you taking the order out of the cardboard waiter's frozen hands and shouting to the kitchen line: "Keep moving! Take this order to the next station!"
            Now that the order line is moving again, Spring Security can finally inspect the order ticket, see that a MANAGER is trying to access the ADMIN kitchen, and reject them with a strict 403 Forbidden!